"""Interview questions for System Design."""

from app.content._build import qa

INTERVIEW = {
    "sd-approach": [
        qa("How do you approach a system design interview question?",
           "Spend the first minutes on requirements: core features, users, scale, latency, availability and consistency needs, and agree what's out of scope. Make quick estimates, define the API and data model, then draw a simple end-to-end design. Next, go deeper into the bottlenecks and failure cases the interviewer cares about, and finish by summarising the trade-offs and what you'd change at ten times the scale. Think out loud and check in with the interviewer as you go.",
           "The same structure works for real design documents at work, where reviewers look for clear requirements, alternatives considered and trade-offs."),
        qa("What are functional and non-functional requirements? Give examples.",
           "Functional requirements describe what the system does: users can post photos, follow others and see a feed. Non-functional requirements describe how well it does it: scale (50 million daily users), performance (the feed loads in under 200 ms at p99), availability (99.95%), consistency (likes can be eventually consistent, payments can't), durability, security and cost. The non-functional requirements usually drive the architecture.",
           "An internal tool and a public social network with identical features end up with completely different designs because of their non-functional requirements."),
        qa("How do you handle ambiguity or missing information?",
           "Ask targeted questions about scale, users and priorities. When the interviewer leaves something open, state a reasonable assumption out loud ('I'll assume 10 million daily users, mostly reading') and move on. Design for your stated assumptions, mention how the design would change if they were different, and cover the core use case before edge features.",
           "Clear assumptions show judgement, and real projects also start with incomplete requirements that you refine as you learn."),
    ],
    "sd-estimation": [
        qa("Estimate the requests per second and storage for a Twitter-like service.",
           "State assumptions: say 200 million daily users, each reading 100 posts and writing 1 a day. Writes: 200M / 86,400 ≈ 2,300 posts a second (about 7,000 at a 3x peak). Reads: 20 billion / 86,400 ≈ 230,000 posts a second, so the system reads 100 times more than it writes, and caching or precomputed feeds are essential. Storage: 200 million posts x about 1 KB ≈ 200 GB a day, or about 73 TB a year before replication, with media kept separately in object storage.",
           "The read-to-write ratio from this estimate drives the key design decision, fan-out on write with cached timelines, covered in the news feed lesson."),
        qa("Why do latency numbers matter in system design?",
           "They show where time goes and which designs are feasible. Memory is roughly 1,000 times faster than an SSD read, and a cross-continent round trip costs around 100 ms, so a page that makes 10 sequential cross-region calls can't load in under a second. That justifies caching hot data in memory, keeping chatty services in the same region, batching or parallelising calls, and serving content close to users with CDNs.",
           "Moving a database to another region without moving its app servers can turn 1 ms queries into 80 ms ones; latency numbers predict that before it happens."),
        qa("How many servers would you need for 50,000 requests per second?",
           "Measure or assume what one server can handle, for example 2,000 simple requests per second from a load test. At peak that's 50,000 / 2,000 = 25 servers. Plan to run at about 60 to 70% utilisation, which means roughly 36 to 42 servers, and add spares across availability zones for failures and deploys. The database is usually the real limit, so also estimate the queries per request and plan caching or replicas for it.",
           "Capacity planning at real companies uses exactly this arithmetic, fed with numbers from load tests rather than guesses."),
    ],
    "sd-load-balancing": [
        qa("Vertical or horizontal scaling: what are the trade-offs?",
           "Vertical scaling means a bigger machine: no code changes and no distributed-systems complexity, but there's a hardware ceiling, costs rise steeply and the machine is a single point of failure. Horizontal scaling adds machines: near-linear capacity, fault tolerance and rolling deploys, but it needs stateless services, load balancing and, for data, replication or sharding. A common path is to scale databases vertically for as long as possible and app servers horizontally from the start.",
           "Stack Overflow famously served huge traffic from a few powerful database servers, while its web tier scaled out behind load balancers."),
        qa("How do you handle user sessions when there are many app servers?",
           "Don't keep sessions in server memory. Use a shared session store such as Redis keyed by a session cookie, or signed (and if needed encrypted) client-side tokens, so servers need no lookup. Sticky sessions at the load balancer are a last resort: they pin a user to one server, which breaks when that server fails and unbalances the load. Shared stores or tokens keep servers stateless.",
           "Autoscaling groups add and remove servers constantly, which only works if no user's session lives on a single machine."),
        qa("What happens when a server behind the load balancer fails?",
           "Health checks (for example GET /health every few seconds) mark it unhealthy after a few failures, and the load balancer stops sending it new requests. Requests already in progress on it fail, and the client or the balancer may retry idempotent ones elsewhere. Connection draining lets servers finish their requests before a planned removal. The load balancer itself is made redundant, as a managed cloud service or an active-passive pair, so it isn't a single point of failure.",
           "Rolling deployments rely on the same mechanism: drain a server, update it, wait for it to pass health checks, then move on to the next."),
    ],
    "sd-caching": [
        qa("Explain cache-aside, write-through and write-back caching.",
           "Cache-aside (lazy loading): the application reads the cache, loads from the database on a miss and fills the cache; writes go to the database and delete the cache key. It's simple and caches only what's used, but first reads are slow and data can be briefly stale. Write-through writes the cache and the database together, so the cache is always fresh, at the cost of slower writes and caching data nobody reads. Write-back writes the cache first and the database later: very fast writes, but data is lost if the cache fails first.",
           "Most web apps use cache-aside with Redis; write-back appears in CPU caches, disk controllers and high-volume counters."),
        qa("How do you keep a cache consistent with the database?",
           "Perfect consistency is hard, so bound the staleness instead. Write to the database first, then delete (rather than update) the cache key, so concurrent writers can't leave an old value behind. Set TTLs so any mistake heals itself, and for stricter needs use versioned keys or change events from the database to invalidate caches. Decide per type of data how much staleness is acceptable.",
           "A product price can be a few seconds stale in search results, but checkout always reads the database."),
        qa("What is a cache stampede, and how do you prevent it?",
           "When a hot key expires or the cache restarts, many requests miss at the same moment and all query the database, which can overload it and cause a cascading failure. Prevent it by coalescing requests or taking a short lock so only one request recomputes the value while the others wait or get the old one, by refreshing popular keys early, by adding random jitter to TTLs so keys don't expire together, and by warming the cache after deploys.",
           "Large sites have suffered outages when a cache cluster restarted and the cold traffic flattened the database, which is why warm-up and coalescing are standard practice."),
    ],
    "sd-rate-limiting": [
        qa("Design a rate limiter for an API.",
           "Clarify the rules: limits per user, API key or IP, per endpoint, and how strict they must be. Use a token bucket or sliding-window counter keyed by client, with counters in Redis updated atomically (a Lua script, or INCR with an expiry) so every API server shares them. Enforce it at the gateway, return 429 with Retry-After and rate-limit headers, and decide whether to fail open or closed if Redis is down. For very high traffic, keep approximate local counters and sync them periodically.",
           "Public APIs such as GitHub's publish per-user limits and return remaining-quota headers so clients can pace themselves."),
        qa("Compare the token bucket and sliding window algorithms.",
           "A token bucket stores two numbers per client (tokens left and the last refill time), allows bursts up to its capacity and enforces a long-run average rate, which suits APIs where short bursts are fine. A sliding window log stores every timestamp and is exact but uses a lot of memory. A sliding window counter estimates the rate from the current and previous window counts using little memory, smoothing out the boundary bursts that fixed windows allow.",
           "Many cloud APIs describe token-bucket-style limits, while login protection often uses sliding windows for steadier enforcement."),
        qa("Should a rate limiter fail open or closed when its store is down?",
           "Failing open (allowing requests) protects availability: users aren't locked out because Redis blinked, but you're unprotected for a while. Failing closed (rejecting requests) protects downstream systems and costs, but turns a limiter outage into a full outage. Many systems fail open for normal endpoints, with local in-memory limits as a fallback, and fail closed for sensitive ones such as login attempts or expensive operations.",
           "This is the kind of trade-off interviewers look for: there's no universal answer, only a choice justified by what each endpoint protects."),
    ],
    "sd-databases": [
        qa("Explain the CAP theorem with an example.",
           "When a network partition splits a distributed data store, it can't provide both consistency (every read returns the latest write) and availability (every node answers). A CP system, such as a bank ledger or a ZooKeeper-style coordination service, refuses or delays requests on one side of the split to stay correct. An AP system, such as a shopping cart or social feed in Cassandra, keeps answering and reconciles later. Without a partition you can have both, but PACELC points out that you still trade latency against consistency.",
           "Real systems choose per feature: account balances need CP behaviour, while like counts are fine being eventually consistent."),
        qa("What is replication lag, and how do you deal with it?",
           "With asynchronous replication, followers apply the leader's changes after a delay, from milliseconds to minutes under load. It causes users not seeing their own writes, data appearing to go backwards when consecutive reads hit different replicas, and lost writes after a failover. Fixes: route a user's reads to the leader after they write, pin a user to one replica for monotonic reads, wait for a replica to reach a known log position, and alert on lag, taking lagging replicas out of rotation.",
           "Many apps send a user's reads to the primary for a few seconds after they post, which hides lag completely for that user."),
        qa("Synchronous or asynchronous replication: what are the trade-offs?",
           "Synchronous replication waits for followers to confirm before acknowledging a write, so no confirmed data is lost on failover, but writes are slower and stall when a follower is slow or down. Asynchronous replication acknowledges once the leader has written, so it's fast and tolerates slow followers, but recent writes can be lost if the leader dies. A common compromise is semi-synchronous: wait for one follower (or a majority) and replicate to the rest asynchronously.",
           "PostgreSQL's synchronous_standby_names setting and MongoDB's w: 'majority' are two real-world versions of this compromise."),
    ],
    "sd-sharding": [
        qa("What is consistent hashing, and why is it useful?",
           "Consistent hashing maps both nodes and keys onto a hash ring; a key belongs to the first node clockwise from its hash. When a node joins or leaves, only the keys between it and its neighbour move, about 1/N of the data, instead of almost everything as with mod-N hashing. Virtual nodes give each physical node many positions on the ring, which evens out the load and spreads a failed node's keys across all the others.",
           "Distributed databases and caches such as Cassandra and DynamoDB use it so clusters can grow or shrink without a massive reshuffle."),
        qa("How do you choose a partition key, and what is a hot partition?",
           "Pick a key with many distinct values that spreads reads and writes evenly and appears in most queries, so each query hits one shard. A hot partition happens when one key or range gets a disproportionate share of traffic: a celebrity's user ID, today's date in a date-ranged table, or a viral product. Fixes include hashing, adding a random suffix (salting) to hot keys and merging on read, caching hot reads, and splitting hot ranges.",
           "Multi-tenant systems often shard by tenant ID but give their largest customers dedicated shards, so one tenant can't overwhelm the others."),
        qa("What problems does sharding introduce?",
           "Queries without the shard key become scatter-gather across every shard. Joins and transactions across shards are slow or unsupported, so you denormalise or use sagas. Unique constraints and auto-increment IDs need global coordination (use UUIDs or Snowflake-style IDs instead). Rebalancing data while serving traffic is complex, and operations, backups and schema changes multiply. That's why teams shard only after vertical scaling, read replicas and caching are no longer enough.",
           "Many companies delay sharding for years by buying bigger database servers and adding replicas, because running a sharded database is a large ongoing cost."),
    ],
    "sd-queues": [
        qa("When would you introduce a message queue?",
           "When work doesn't need to finish within the user's request (emails, thumbnails, reports); when traffic is bursty and consumers should work at a steady rate; when several services must react to the same event without the producer knowing them; or when a slow or unreliable downstream system shouldn't take the caller down with it. The costs are eventual consistency, harder debugging and new failure modes, so don't add one where a direct call is fine.",
           "Checkout flows typically confirm the order synchronously, then push receipts, analytics and warehouse updates onto queues."),
        qa("How do you achieve exactly-once processing?",
           "Networks make exactly-once delivery impossible in general, so you aim for exactly-once effects: at-least-once delivery plus idempotent processing. Give each message a unique ID and record processed IDs in the same transaction as the side effect, or use naturally idempotent writes such as upserts and conditional updates. On the producer side, use a transactional outbox so an event is published if and only if the database change commits.",
           "Payment systems pass idempotency keys end to end, so a retried charge request never bills a customer twice."),
        qa("What is the outbox pattern?",
           "Writing to a database and then publishing to a broker are two separate operations, so a crash between them loses or duplicates events (the dual-write problem). With an outbox, the service writes its business change and an event row to an outbox table in the same database transaction. A relay process, or change-data-capture with a tool such as Debezium, reads the outbox, publishes the events and marks them sent. Consumers stay idempotent, because a relay may publish an event twice.",
           "Microservices that must reliably tell others about changes, such as 'order placed' events driving payment and shipping, use the outbox pattern to avoid lost events."),
    ],
    "sd-reliability": [
        qa("How can retries make an outage worse, and how do you retry safely?",
           "If every layer retries three times, one failing call at the bottom of a five-layer stack can become 3^5 = 243 attempts, and synchronised retries hit a struggling service exactly when it's weakest (a retry storm). Retry safely: only idempotent operations or those with idempotency keys, only temporary errors (timeouts, 503, or 429 after Retry-After), with capped exponential backoff plus jitter, within a retry budget (say at most 10% extra traffic), at one layer only, and with circuit breakers that stop when a dependency is clearly down.",
           "AWS's published guidance on timeouts, retries and backoff with jitter grew out of exactly these cascading-failure lessons."),
        qa("Explain SLIs, SLOs, SLAs and error budgets.",
           "An SLI is a measured indicator of service health, such as the share of successful requests or of requests faster than 300 ms. An SLO is the internal target for an SLI over a time window, such as 99.9% over 30 days. An SLA is an external contract with consequences, such as refunds, and is usually looser than the SLO. The error budget is 100% minus the SLO: teams spend it on risky releases and, when it runs out, prioritise reliability work.",
           "Google's SRE practice popularised error budgets as a way to balance shipping speed against reliability with data instead of arguments."),
        qa("What is a circuit breaker, and when do you use one?",
           "A circuit breaker wraps calls to a remote dependency and counts failures. When failures pass a threshold, it opens and fails immediately, protecting both the caller's resources and the struggling dependency. After a cool-down it goes half-open and lets a trial request through: success closes it, failure opens it again. Use one around any network dependency whose failure shouldn't cascade, and pair it with a fallback such as cached data or a default response.",
           "A product page can show items without personalised recommendations while the recommendation service's breaker is open, instead of failing the whole page."),
    ],
    "sd-microservices": [
        qa("Monolith or microservices: how do you decide?",
           "Microservices help when many teams need to deploy independently, when parts of the system have very different scaling or technology needs, or when failure isolation matters. They cost network unreliability, distributed data, harder testing and debugging, and heavy platform investment (CI/CD, observability, service discovery). For a new product or a small team, a modular monolith is usually faster and safer; extract services along proven boundaries when team size or scaling pain justifies it.",
           "Shopify runs one of the largest Ruby on Rails modular monoliths, while companies such as Netflix moved to microservices so hundreds of teams could ship independently."),
        qa("What is the saga pattern?",
           "A saga manages a business transaction that spans several services, each with its own database. It runs a sequence of local transactions, and if one fails it runs compensating transactions for the steps already completed (refund the payment, release the stock). With orchestration, a central coordinator tells each service what to do; with choreography, services react to each other's events. Sagas are eventually consistent, so design for intermediate states such as a 'pending' order, and make every step idempotent.",
           "Travel bookings are a classic saga: reserve the flight, hotel and car, and cancel the earlier reservations if a later one fails."),
        qa("How do services find and call each other reliably?",
           "Service discovery (DNS in Kubernetes, Consul, or a cloud load balancer) maps service names to healthy instances. Calls use timeouts, retries with backoff, circuit breakers and authentication between services (mutual TLS or signed tokens), often provided by a service mesh such as Istio or Linkerd. Contracts are versioned (OpenAPI or Protocol Buffers) so services can deploy independently, and distributed tracing ties each request together across services.",
           "In Kubernetes, an orders pod simply calls http://payments:8080, and cluster DNS plus the service object route the request to a healthy payments pod."),
    ],
    "sd-url-shortener": [
        qa("Design a URL shortener like bit.ly.",
           "Clarify the features (create, redirect, custom aliases, expiry, analytics) and the scale (say 100 million links a month, 100 reads per write). Estimates: about 40 writes and 4,000 redirects a second, and about 6 TB over 10 years. API: POST /links, and GET /{code} returning a 302. Store code -> URL in a key-value store or a SQL table sharded by code. Generate codes from a distributed counter (blocks of IDs) encoded in base62 and scrambled so they can't be listed in order, or use random codes with collision checks. Put Redis and a CDN in front for redirects, send click events to a queue for analytics, and add rate limits and malware checks.",
           "This problem rehearses the core toolkit (estimates, ID generation, caching, sharding, asynchronous analytics) that every larger design reuses."),
        qa("How would you generate unique IDs in a distributed system?",
           "Options: database auto-increment (simple, but one bottleneck); blocks of IDs handed out by a central service so each server allocates locally; UUIDs (no coordination, but 128 bits, and random order hurts index locality; UUIDv7 is time-ordered); and Snowflake-style 64-bit IDs combining a millisecond timestamp, a machine ID and a per-millisecond sequence, which sort roughly by time and need no coordination. Choose based on size, ordering and whether IDs may be guessable.",
           "Twitter's Snowflake and Instagram's sharded ID scheme are well-known production examples, and many databases now support time-ordered UUIDv7."),
        qa("How do you handle a link that suddenly gets millions of clicks?",
           "Redirects are easy to cache: the hot code sits in Redis and in the CDN, so the database barely notices. Replicate very hot keys across cache nodes, or keep a small in-process cache on each app server, so one Redis shard isn't overloaded. Record clicks asynchronously and aggregate them (count per minute in memory, then flush) instead of writing a database row per click. Autoscale the stateless redirect servers and protect them with rate limits against abuse.",
           "Links shared during big live events or in viral posts create exactly this hot-key pattern, which is why redirect services rely so heavily on edge caching."),
    ],
    "sd-news-feed": [
        qa("Design the news feed for a social network.",
           "Requirements: post, follow, and view a feed of followed accounts' posts, newest first or ranked, loading in about 200 ms for hundreds of millions of users, with far more reads than writes. Store posts in a sharded store and the follow graph in its own service. Use hybrid fan-out: when a normal account posts, a queue-driven fan-out service pushes the post ID into each follower's cached feed list (capped at a few hundred), while celebrity posts are pulled and merged at read time. The feed service reads IDs, loads the posts from a cache in batches, ranks them and returns a cursor. Media goes through object storage and a CDN.",
           "Twitter, Instagram and Facebook have all described variants of this hybrid design as their user numbers grew."),
        qa("Fan-out on write or fan-out on read: what are the trade-offs?",
           "Fan-out on write precomputes each follower's feed when a post is created: reads are very fast, but write work grows with the follower count, celebrity posts take a long time to spread, and inactive users' feeds waste work and storage. Fan-out on read builds the feed at request time: writes are cheap and feeds always fresh, but reads are slow and expensive for users who follow many accounts. A hybrid keeps reads fast for everyone while capping the cost of celebrity posts.",
           "Interviewers expect you to spot the celebrity problem and propose the hybrid; missing it is a common reason feed designs fall short."),
        qa("How would you rank a feed instead of sorting it by time?",
           "Gather candidates (recent posts from followed accounts, plus some recommendations), compute features (how close the user is to the author, engagement so far, content type, recency, the user's past behaviour) and score them with a model that predicts engagement. Then apply rules for diversity, freshness and integrity, such as down-ranking spam. Run it in stages: cheap filtering of thousands of candidates, then a heavier model on the top few hundred. Judge changes with A/B tests on long-term metrics, not just clicks.",
           "Every major social app ranks feeds this way, and the same 'retrieve, then rank' pattern also powers search and recommendations."),
    ],
    "sd-chat": [
        qa("Design a chat system like WhatsApp or Slack.",
           "Requirements: one-to-one and group chat, online presence, delivery and read receipts, multi-device sync, push notifications for offline users and message history, all with low latency at large scale. Clients hold WebSocket connections to stateless chat servers behind a load balancer, and a session service records which server each user is connected to. The message service assigns per-conversation sequence IDs, stores messages in a write-optimised store partitioned by conversation, then routes them to recipients' servers through pub/sub, or to a push-notification service if they're offline. Devices sync from their last seen ID when they reconnect, large groups fan out through queues, and media goes to object storage and a CDN.",
           "This design combines most of the course (load balancing, sharding, queues, idempotency and caching), which is why it's a popular senior-level interview question."),
        qa("How do you keep chat messages in the right order?",
           "Clocks on different devices disagree, so the server decides the order. A per-conversation sequence number (from the partition that owns the conversation, or a counter in the database) gives a total order within each chat, and time-ordered IDs such as Snowflake give a near-order across the whole system. Clients sort by these IDs, show their own unconfirmed messages separately until the server acknowledges them, and detect gaps (receiving 41 after 39) to fetch the missing message.",
           "Slack and Discord both order messages by server-assigned, time-sortable IDs, which also makes 'load older messages' a simple range query."),
        qa("How would you implement online presence?",
           "Each connected client sends a heartbeat every few seconds, and the presence service stores 'last seen' with a short TTL in a fast store such as Redis, marking a user offline when heartbeats stop instead of relying on clean disconnects. Publish presence changes only to the users who care (contacts or open chats), and batch or throttle the updates, because presence changes far more often than messages are sent. In huge groups, load presence lazily, when someone opens the member list.",
           "Presence is a classic hidden scaling cost: one user coming online can notify hundreds of contacts, so production systems throttle and scope presence updates carefully."),
    ],
}
